WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:07.799 --> 00:00:13.490
Sir Malcolm Grant: Good morning everybody
and welcome to this meeting of the Board of

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:19.170
NHS England. I extend a particular welcome
to members of the public who have joined us

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:25.589
in the room here in London this morning, and
of course, as everybody is aware, these meetings

00:00:25.589 --> 00:00:32.270
are also livestreamed. That is a fundamental
commitment by this Board from the very beginning

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:38.680
to the principles of openness and transparency;
we believe that the public of England have

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:45.680
a right to see how and why decisions affecting
the investments that we make in the NHS are

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:52.980
made. So, a warm welcome to everybody.
I should stress that the meeting of the Board

00:00:53.089 --> 00:00:58.940
this morning is a meeting of the Board in
public, and as opposed to a public meeting,

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:05.680
by the way, this is a business meeting of
the Board which is observed in public. We

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:10.200
will also, at the end of this meeting, resolve
to go into a private session to deal with

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:17.200
material which by virtue of its confidentiality
or commercial sensitivity we are obliged to

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:24.330
deal with in private. I should also say that
the Board meets formally in public but also

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:30.680
does quite a lot of its work through strategy
meetings and informal discussions, so a wide

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:36.370
array of meetings with stakeholders, with
patients groups and with others who have an

00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:41.510
acute interest in the work that we do, so
that by the time much of the business comes

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:47.470
to the Board there has been an elaborate process
of strategic development to try to crystallise

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:51.600
the propositions that come to the Board.
Through much of that work the Board works

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:56.920
as a unitary Board; we have non executive
members and we have executive members, but

00:01:56.920 --> 00:02:02.450
in terms of strategic responsibility and indeed
governance responsibility for the work of

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:09.450
NHS England, this is a unified unitary Board.
The two formal issues that I need to raise

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:18.810
with you now please, one is there is no fire
alarm scheduled for this morning, so in the

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:25.810
event that an alarm goes off our staff will
safely escort you off the premises, and two,

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:32.300
to ask you please everybody if you could switch
off mobile phones so that there is no interference

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:35.260
with the proceedings.
Formalities

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:40.690
I am now then going to declare the meeting
formally open. I am going to invite any members

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:46.450
of the Board to declare any interests that
they have in matters on the agenda that are

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:53.450
not otherwise registered. I have no declarations
of interest. I wish formally to report one

00:02:54.210 --> 00:03:01.210
apology for absence which is David Roberts,
who unfortunately has an unavoidable clash

00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:07.900
of Board responsibilities. Next, I would like
formally this morning to welcome Fiona Barr,

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:14.350
sitting on my right, who is the new Board
secretary responsible for the oversight of

00:03:14.350 --> 00:03:21.350
governance across NHS England and for ensuring
that the transaction of business by the Board

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:29.280
is properly and effectively organised.
Next, to ask the Board whether there are any

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:37.200
requests for me to un star the items of business
in item ten. I have had no prior notification,

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:42.950
and I gather then we will take those as read,
but I know, knowing my Board well, that if

00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:47.130
anybody changes their mind during the meeting
they will make that apparent to me.

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:50.170
Minutes of the previous meeting
Okay, let us then move on into the formal

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:56.620
business of the meeting. We have the minutes
of the last meeting; I have had no queries

00:03:56.620 --> 00:04:01.970
on them since we first issued them. Can I
take them as a true and accurate record? Thank

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:08.970
you very much. We have, as I understand it,
no matters arising that we should note, but

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:17.150
there are various actions that we know are
underway. Let me then turn please to item

00:04:17.150 --> 00:04:20.239
three, which is the Chief Executive's report.
Simon.

00:04:20.239 --> 00:04:24.860
Simon Stevens: Thank you, Malcolm. I am going
to be very brief because I will pick up each

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:31.759
of the succeeding Board papers, and most of
my report relates to those items, other than

00:04:31.759 --> 00:04:38.759
to say that since we last met the Chancellor
has allocated additional funding for the NHS,

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:44.689
as you know, and so one of our principal duties
here today is to make decisions about how

00:04:44.689 --> 00:04:51.689
that money is allocated per the paper before
us later. Very effective joint work has continued

00:04:52.479 --> 00:04:57.759
since the publication of the NHS Five Year
Forward View, with the other five national

00:04:57.759 --> 00:05:03.330
leadership bodies in the NHS, CQC, Health
Education England, Public Health England,

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:09.180
Monitor, TDA, and when we come onto the discussion
about the planning guidance, or year one of

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:13.680
the Forward View as we are styling it for
next year, we will have a chance to reflect

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:18.919
further on that.
The government has also now published the

00:05:18.919 --> 00:05:24.110
mandate for NHS England which effectively
sets the goal through the democratic process

00:05:24.110 --> 00:05:30.889
for the NHS for 2015/16, and as expected,
that for the most part represents continuity

00:05:30.889 --> 00:05:35.270
with the objectives that the Health Service
was being asked to pursue this year, with

00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:42.050
some enhancements including on mental health
services. So when we come to that item we

00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:44.879
will have chance to reflect specifically on
that.

00:05:44.879 --> 00:05:51.879
Then obviously at the same as people are planning
for next year and beyond, there is also intense

00:05:52.069 --> 00:05:56.259
work across the National Health Service and
local government to ensure that patients continue

00:05:56.259 --> 00:06:01.860
to get safe, high quality services through
urgent and emergency care over this winter

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:06.800
period, so Barbara will update the Board on
that specifically.

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:10.819
I am happy to take any other questions on
matters not coming up subsequently, otherwise

00:06:10.819 --> 00:06:12.279
we can proceed.
Q&amp;A

00:06:12.279 --> 00:06:16.669
Sir Malcolm Grant: Anything for the Chief
Executive at this stage?

00:06:16.669 --> 00:06:21.960
Speaker: Just to say I think [inaudible].
I like the additions but I like continuity

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:24.860
as well.
Sir Malcolm Grant: Thank you, noted. Right,

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:31.860
we have got some very gritty items of business
on the agenda today, so let us move forward

00:06:32.509 --> 00:06:37.400
with the first of these which is the planning
guidance. Simon, did you want to say some

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:41.199
further words about that before we call upon
Ian to introduce the paper?

00:06:41.199 --> 00:06:46.999
Simon Stevens: So, this will be the first
time ever that the annual planning guidance

00:06:46.999 --> 00:06:53.159
for the National Health Service has been a
shared enterprise of all of the national leadership

00:06:53.159 --> 00:07:00.159
bodies in the NHS. We aim to publish this
on Friday and our colleagues at TDA, Monitor

00:07:01.419 --> 00:07:06.559
and the other bodies as well have been intimately
involved with us, together with the guiding

00:07:06.559 --> 00:07:11.749
coalition that brought together the Forward
View in shaping what this will look like.

00:07:11.749 --> 00:07:18.029
So this is not just business as usual; this
is going to be firing the starting gun on

00:07:18.029 --> 00:07:23.629
a number of the work streams that we identified
in the Forward View, and Ian is going to give

00:07:23.629 --> 00:07:30.139
more of a flavour of that. Obviously we will
have a chance with others to make final adjustments

00:07:30.139 --> 00:07:31.639
to this before publication on Friday.

00:07:31.639 --> 00:07:36.169
Ian Dodge: Thank you very much Simon. So,
it is a collaborative endeavour with the other

00:07:36.169 --> 00:07:42.330
five ALB partners, and a wide range of other
national stakeholders. That partnership will

00:07:42.330 --> 00:07:45.869
extend not just to the production of the planning
guidance, but actually taking forward its

00:07:45.869 --> 00:07:52.869
contents during 2015/2016 and the assurance
of plans jointly by the arm's length bodies

00:07:53.629 --> 00:08:00.629
in what marks, we hope, a departure from previous
practice. The two key themes of the planning

00:08:03.279 --> 00:08:10.279
guidance are progressing the Forward View
in 2015/2016 is the first year, as Simon

00:08:10.940 --> 00:08:17.940
has alluded to and also integrating that
with a continued need for a forensic focus

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:24.759
on operational performance and delivering
the commitments within the mandate, the continuation

00:08:24.759 --> 00:08:30.490
of those objectives which were set previously
that the NHS needs to continue to deliver

00:08:30.490 --> 00:08:37.460
against, as well as then the one substantive
change which is the introduction of waiting

00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:44.050
times standards for mental health services.
Content

00:08:44.050 --> 00:08:50.240
In running through the content very briefly
of what that joint guidance will look like,

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:57.240
I would like to emphasise just a few points.
The first is the importance that all of the

00:08:57.459 --> 00:09:03.240
arm's length bodies are placing on what was
chapter two of the Forward View: the need

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:09.490
for NHS organisations to get serious about
prevention, working with our local government

00:09:09.490 --> 00:09:15.620
partners and voluntary sector partners on
engaging communities and empowering patients,

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:22.620
and placing that right at the heart of planning
requirements for 2015/2016.

00:09:22.850 --> 00:09:29.850
The second point I would like to pick up on
content is around the progression of work

00:09:29.990 --> 00:09:36.990
on new models of care, where we are looking
to work intensively with Vanguard sites to

00:09:39.259 --> 00:09:45.630
provide some support to help them get over
the line as quickly as possible as well as

00:09:45.630 --> 00:09:52.630
then work with health economies which face
the greatest difficulties as part of a new

00:09:53.300 --> 00:10:00.300
success regime to help kick start transformation
in those areas. More widely, we are looking

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:09.100
to have a more permissive regime for the rest
of the country including an invitation that

00:10:12.519 --> 00:10:18.149
local economies working in a collaborative
way could bring in national partners to offer

00:10:18.149 --> 00:10:25.110
insight and value to the local plans that
they are constructing.

00:10:25.110 --> 00:10:30.350
Then the final point I would like to pick
up on is in relation to delivering improved

00:10:30.350 --> 00:10:35.589
outcomes, in particular patient safety, and
I think it is noteworthy that we will be introducing

00:10:35.589 --> 00:10:42.589
in 2015/2016 a new national CQUIN indicator
for tackling sepsis, and also a new national

00:10:46.940 --> 00:10:53.940
indicator for tackling acute kidney illness.
We will be able to do that through the planned

00:10:54.379 --> 00:11:01.079
retiring of the existing Safety Thermometer
indicator and the Friends and Family Test

00:11:01.079 --> 00:11:08.079
indicator in relation to hospital services,
both of which will find themselves in revisions

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:14.110
to the standard NHS contract.

00:11:14.110 --> 00:11:19.160
Sir Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much for
that succinct summary. Let me invite comments

00:11:19.160 --> 00:11:21.699
and questions from the Board. Victor.
Victor Adebowale: Now that I have got the

00:11:21.699 --> 00:11:27.029
right version, I can congratulate you on the
fact that you have mentioned NHS Citizens.

00:11:27.029 --> 00:11:32.980
There are three things actually I want to
say. The first is that the NHS Citizens point

00:11:32.980 --> 00:11:38.839
that is made in the engaging communities is
a significant shift I think in the way we

00:11:38.839 --> 00:11:44.790
do things around here. I think that we are
going to have to work really hard to get the

00:11:44.790 --> 00:11:51.579
skills and the organisational design right
to ensure that NHS Citizens impacts on areas

00:11:51.579 --> 00:11:55.440
like commissioning, and I think it will come
up again in the course of this meeting.

00:11:55.440 --> 00:12:01.560
The second thing I think is in the report,
is in the guidance, but it is kind of skirted

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:06.829
over, which is the Race Equality Standard.
I know that it will be seen as a minor intervention;

00:12:06.829 --> 00:12:12.399
I think it is a significant patient safety
intervention, and if I have a question, it

00:12:12.399 --> 00:12:18.439
will be about how we ensure that it is a system
wide intervention that has the system wide

00:12:18.439 --> 00:12:25.439
leadership across it, which you talked about.
The third point is that the voluntary sector

00:12:26.529 --> 00:12:33.529
is mentioned I think in this version in relation
to grant and grant agreements. I think I would

00:12:33.949 --> 00:12:40.949
want to comment on the role of the voluntary
sector beyond the grant agreement. I would

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:49.240
declare my interest in that the voluntary
sector is a significant provider of some services

00:12:49.420 --> 00:12:56.160
which are contracted to the NHS, and there
is a question about how the not for profit

00:12:56.160 --> 00:12:59.350
sector, voluntary sector, call it what
you like , actually engages in the health

00:12:59.350 --> 00:13:06.149
and social care market at the moment beyond
the grant arrangements. So in a sense it does

00:13:06.149 --> 00:13:09.290
not go far enough; it is welcome that it is
there, but I think there are issues about

00:13:09.290 --> 00:13:14.769
the marketplace and not for profit providers
that go beyond just the arrangement of grants.

00:13:14.769 --> 00:13:21.019
Sir Malcolm Grant: If you would like to respond.
Ian Dodge: I think your first point on the

00:13:21.019 --> 00:13:28.019
NHS system is extremely well made. As you
say, we will need to embed public engagement,

00:13:28.949 --> 00:13:35.189
engagement of communities, as an integral
part of commissioning.

00:13:35.189 --> 00:13:41.129
Your second point on the introduction of a
new workforce Race Equality Standard  and

00:13:41.129 --> 00:13:47.370
this follows discussion and consideration
by the National Equality and Diversity Council

00:13:47.370 --> 00:13:53.889
earlier in the year  is as you say a very
significant intervention not just for staff,

00:13:53.889 --> 00:13:59.949
but we hope for users of services as we end
up with the leadership of NHS organisations

00:13:59.949 --> 00:14:06.949
better reflecting the communities that they
serve. We are very much planning to work in

00:14:07.180 --> 00:14:14.180
NHS England with the constituent members of
the EDC; indeed, I wrote out to EDC members

00:14:14.560 --> 00:14:21.560
last week inviting their contributions as
part of a broad based coalition to try and

00:14:23.709 --> 00:14:30.149
drive action in this area.
Then thirdly, again I would agree wholeheartedly

00:14:30.149 --> 00:14:37.129
with your observation around the importance
of the new arrangements that we are planning

00:14:37.129 --> 00:14:44.129
for grants which are simpler than going through
the bureaucracy of the national Standard Contract,

00:14:44.829 --> 00:14:51.629
and the need for us to supplement that with
both the invitation for voluntary sector leadership

00:14:51.629 --> 00:14:57.269
of many of the aspects of the Forward View
work, and indeed thinking not just about their

00:14:57.269 --> 00:15:04.269
leadership nationally in relation to policy
and strategy and programme design, but their

00:15:04.470 --> 00:15:11.459
role as a contributor delivering services
to communities.

00:15:11.459 --> 00:15:16.129
Sir Malcolm Grant: Noel.
Noel Gordon: Thank you Malcolm. Given that

00:15:16.129 --> 00:15:22.339
one of the central planks of the Five Year
Forward View is to consider and introduce

00:15:22.339 --> 00:15:29.339
new models of care, what is your feeling Ian
about the pace and urgency with which we can

00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:35.769
capitalise on the groundswell generated and
support generated in the Five Year Forward

00:15:35.769 --> 00:15:42.769
View for new models of care, and also capitalise
on the Dalton Review I think which was around

00:15:43.019 --> 00:15:50.019
the organisational constructs which came out
a week or so ago? How can we inject that urgency

00:15:50.100 --> 00:15:57.100
into the system, Ian?
Ian Dodge: So, I think again you make a very

00:15:58.800 --> 00:16:05.800
good point around the level of enthusiasm
that has been generated by the Forward View,

00:16:07.499 --> 00:16:13.499
the different conversations that are happening
within individual organisations but also potentially

00:16:13.499 --> 00:16:20.499
more critically across local health economies
as communities are beginning to think through

00:16:21.529 --> 00:16:26.800
actually what might the Forward View mean
for them. I think our challenge in the planning

00:16:26.800 --> 00:16:33.800
guidance and then the subsequent rapid mobilisation
of national resource across multiple agencies

00:16:35.569 --> 00:16:42.569
will be able to provide a compelling offer
of support to Vanguard sites across the country

00:16:46.079 --> 00:16:52.209
to help unblock some of the barriers to change
that people are experiencing, whether this

00:16:52.209 --> 00:16:57.899
is information governance, challenges around
navigating procurement rules, understanding

00:16:57.899 --> 00:17:04.899
constraints around the workforce or a desire
to develop new contractual and payment mechanisms.

00:17:08.140 --> 00:17:13.120
We have I think a major programme of work
that we will be constructing very early on

00:17:13.120 --> 00:17:19.410
in the New Year to help address those issues,
and we will need to coproduce that with our

00:17:19.410 --> 00:17:24.380
arm's length body partners, with our wider
coalition of other national partners, and

00:17:24.380 --> 00:17:31.380
above all jointly with the local sites who
are experiencing these difficulties.

00:17:33.700 --> 00:17:35.750
Sir Malcolm Grant: Tim.
Tim Kelsey: Thanks Malcolm. I just wanted

00:17:35.750 --> 00:17:39.490
to bring to the Board's attention, a theme
which emerges in this planning guidance which

00:17:39.490 --> 00:17:46.490
I think will come to be regarded as one of
the most important step changes we are engaged

00:17:47.410 --> 00:17:52.880
in in relation to modernising services, and
that is the focus it has on really forcing

00:17:52.880 --> 00:17:58.620
the pace of adoption of core digital standards
in our provider services. This is really just

00:17:58.620 --> 00:18:03.460
for people the first time that there has been
a consensus right across the system, the health

00:18:03.460 --> 00:18:10.460
and care system, that we now need to bring
real focus to delivery of a safe, sustainable

00:18:11.360 --> 00:18:18.360
digital service, not just in relation to the
improvement of delivery of services, but also

00:18:19.130 --> 00:18:23.110
to put patients much more fundamentally in
control of what they do. So, for example the

00:18:23.110 --> 00:18:28.970
planning guidance notes and encourages the
adoption during the course of 2015/2016 of

00:18:28.970 --> 00:18:33.090
people's access to their own data in primary
care and elsewhere, but I just wanted to bring

00:18:33.090 --> 00:18:37.330
that to people's attention because I think
in many ways it underpins much of what the

00:18:37.330 --> 00:18:41.330
Forward View aspires to deliver, but also
it is I think a very important step forward

00:18:41.330 --> 00:18:48.330
in relation to driving change in the NHS.
Speaker: Malcolm, I just wanted to underline

00:18:55.200 --> 00:18:59.000
the point that Simon made about the continuing
collaboration that this represents. It is

00:18:59.000 --> 00:19:04.280
incredibly important that people do not design
the future collaboratively and then go off

00:19:04.280 --> 00:19:11.040
and do their own different things, giving
different messages to the wider sector, so

00:19:11.040 --> 00:19:17.640
I know it will not be easy for Simon and his
colleagues in NHS England but also for those

00:19:17.640 --> 00:19:24.130
other bodies. So, I think it is important
that we recognise and support the centrality

00:19:24.130 --> 00:19:28.760
of the fact that if this difficult job is
not done collaboratively it really will not

00:19:28.760 --> 00:19:32.190
get done.
Sir Malcolm Grant: I think that is the minimal

00:19:32.190 --> 00:19:37.200
expectation that this service has of us actually,
is that the leading arm's length body should

00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:41.550
work together. It is not straightforward,
because we each have different responsibilities,

00:19:41.550 --> 00:19:46.830
different focuses in different parts of the
service, but I think one of the bedevilling

00:19:46.830 --> 00:19:49.340
characteristics of past history is different
messages coming from different arm's length

00:19:49.340 --> 00:19:55.380
bodies and from government, and if we can
be united about this, I think that is a fundamental

00:19:55.380 --> 00:19:59.790
precondition to the Forward View actually
working. Ciarán.

00:19:59.790 --> 00:20:06.240
Ciarán Devane: I agree with all of that,
by the way. My way is change capability on

00:20:06.240 --> 00:20:09.940
the ground; the system is running around faster
than ever, demand higher than ever, people

00:20:09.940 --> 00:20:16.330
growing up in a culture in which the boundaries
of their role and their institution have been

00:20:16.330 --> 00:20:22.540
fairly well defined. Apart from a great document
and exhortation, what specifically are we

00:20:22.540 --> 00:20:27.110
going to do to make sure that there is change
capability out there and collaboration can

00:20:27.110 --> 00:20:33.700
happen in each of the health economies that
we are talking about? We can hope for it,

00:20:33.700 --> 00:20:37.910
but that will not actually work.
Ian Dodge: I think you have highlighted there

00:20:37.910 --> 00:20:44.910
Ciarn a profound challenge that we collectively
face here. I am confident that, working with

00:20:45.700 --> 00:20:51.530
our partners, we can develop a national support
programme which can particularly help the

00:20:51.530 --> 00:20:58.530
Vanguard sites and some of the economies where
we will need to take a more directive approach.

00:21:00.530 --> 00:21:07.530
We have the reviews of some of the national
support arrangements so IQ, leadership

00:21:08.300 --> 00:21:15.040
academy, academic science networks and so
on which will need to be aligned with

00:21:15.040 --> 00:21:20.680
this agenda, and I think that will potentially
provide part of the answer.

00:21:20.680 --> 00:21:27.680
Part of the answer as well, I think, will
be the way in which different existing organisations

00:21:29.350 --> 00:21:36.220
decide to collaborate and use networked approaches.
So Noel, you raised the point around the Dalton

00:21:36.220 --> 00:21:42.160
Review; some of the recommendations of the
Dalton Review are around the development of

00:21:42.160 --> 00:21:49.160
management chains and franchises, whether
that is tertiary chains or whole organisational

00:21:52.300 --> 00:21:57.810
chains, and I think there will certainly be
scope for organisations to build additional

00:21:57.810 --> 00:22:04.250
capability for change management through more
formal or informal partnership approaches

00:22:04.250 --> 00:22:11.250
across different organisations.
But I think we need to recognise that a profound

00:22:11.780 --> 00:22:18.780
change in the delivery of models of care that
reflects, picking up on Tim's point, the transformative

00:22:19.890 --> 00:22:26.890
potential of technology as well as redesigning
workforce roles, the offer of care to individuals

00:22:27.440 --> 00:22:33.830
that breaks down barriers between formal caregiving
and the role of communities and their families,

00:22:33.830 --> 00:22:40.830
as well as then a set of major technical enablers
and organisational design challenges is a

00:22:42.080 --> 00:22:49.080
profound long term programme for change. So
whilst I hope we can make rapid progress in

00:22:50.710 --> 00:22:57.340
demonstrating success and demonstrating success
with sites that are not just solving their

00:22:57.340 --> 00:23:04.340
own problems but actually serving as replicable
prototypes for others, and the subsequent

00:23:04.590 --> 00:23:11.370
wave of early adopters, we need to be realistic
about the pace of whole system transformation

00:23:11.370 --> 00:23:18.040
and the amount of investment that will be
required to deliver that consistently across

00:23:18.040 --> 00:23:21.300
the NHS.
Sir Malcolm Grant: Whilst being not top down

00:23:21.300 --> 00:23:25.570
and directive, and whilst developing local
leadership and capacity.

00:23:25.570 --> 00:23:29.860
Ian Dodge: Exactly, sir Chair.
Sir Malcolm Grant: Ed.

00:23:29.860 --> 00:23:35.430
Ed Smith: So, we can say all that, and we
have to say all that, and the challenge is

00:23:35.430 --> 00:23:41.910
the complexity of it. I think we are so massively
supportive of the guiding coalition that has

00:23:41.910 --> 00:23:47.610
been created, and of course accountability
and structures that are separate is important,

00:23:47.610 --> 00:23:54.610
but the further we go with this the more porosity
of boundaries is necessary. It is not going

00:23:56.910 --> 00:24:02.500
to be just reviews of bodies that fix it;
it is going to be how people operate at all

00:24:02.500 --> 00:24:06.750
levels on the ground day in, day out, how
they relate to one another, how they respect

00:24:06.750 --> 00:24:11.240
each other, how they focus on the outcomes
of the work that they are doing together.

00:24:11.240 --> 00:24:18.240
This is a monumental behavioural change which
does not start in the centre, but signals

00:24:20.440 --> 00:24:27.440
can be sent through a variety of things underpinned
by enablers around data. So, Simon: I think

00:24:29.170 --> 00:24:36.170
at the top level the guiding coalition is
there, and sort of there emotionally and intellectually.

00:24:37.760 --> 00:24:44.760
How do we accelerate the creation of that
guiding coalition at every level in the organisation

00:24:45.400 --> 00:24:50.660
such that when we come through to this planning
guidance which is about to be live, we get

00:24:50.660 --> 00:24:53.810
a shift in the way in which things come together
and get done?

00:24:53.810 --> 00:24:58.630
Simon Stevens: Yes, well in terms of the statutory
agencies, the six statutory agencies, obviously

00:24:58.630 --> 00:25:04.070
as you say there is the national piece. The
regional groupings are coming together in

00:25:04.070 --> 00:25:09.590
a much more coherent way than has been the
case before. Obviously NHS England has more

00:25:09.590 --> 00:25:14.920
folks regionally than does TDA, and TDA has
more than Monitor, so there is a sort of sense

00:25:14.920 --> 00:25:19.260
of shared endeavour there. Locally obviously
what we are going to be doing through the

00:25:19.260 --> 00:25:24.740
process that Ian just described in terms of
asking those parts of the country that think

00:25:24.740 --> 00:25:30.420
they are on the cusp of doing something really
dramatic and exciting is then applying a series

00:25:30.420 --> 00:25:35.840
of tests and supports, and part of that is
show us that you are working collectively,

00:25:35.840 --> 00:25:40.780
show us that you are mobilising and engaging
more broadly than just the Health Service,

00:25:40.780 --> 00:25:45.550
not also just the local government, but actually
making a reality of the notion of, We have

00:25:45.550 --> 00:25:51.710
got to mobilise community groups, faith groups,
the voluntary sector, patients organisations

00:25:51.710 --> 00:25:57.660
and others across your community for the direction
that you want to chart.'

00:25:57.660 --> 00:26:02.900
For the second group however, those that are
kind of in sustained structural operational

00:26:02.900 --> 00:26:09.130
financial problems, people in a way for different
reasons know they need to change. What is

00:26:09.130 --> 00:26:13.130
remarkable is when you look at these places,
it is the same places in many cases that for

00:26:13.130 --> 00:26:19.550
five, ten, 15 years have had these deep seated
problems, and so that is why I think people

00:26:19.550 --> 00:26:23.990
are willing to now say, as I said before,
Since we cannot solve the exam questions,

00:26:23.990 --> 00:26:28.270
as opposed to asking a different set of exam
questions let us just put more stuff into

00:26:28.270 --> 00:26:31.970
contention,' and part of what they have got
to put in contention is what you have just

00:26:31.970 --> 00:26:35.410
described. So, I think there is a willingness
at both ends of the spectrum, even if it is

00:26:35.410 --> 00:26:40.520
motivated by different sets of reasons. Since
we cannot work collectively with everybody

00:26:40.520 --> 00:26:44.880
 we would be spreading ourselves very thin
 that is why we have made the kind of decisions

00:26:44.880 --> 00:26:49.970
to do those who need it most for the new successful
regime, and those who are nearest to pulling

00:26:49.970 --> 00:26:54.600
it off to back them as they do it.
Ian Dodge: If I can add just very briefly,

00:26:54.600 --> 00:27:01.170
I think this will call for a different scale
of engagement between national organisations

00:27:01.170 --> 00:27:08.110
and their regional outposts than has hitherto
been the case, and again a different intensity

00:27:08.110 --> 00:27:12.650
of communications with the NHS.
Sir Malcolm Grant: One thing I think we as

00:27:12.650 --> 00:27:17.870
a Board would seek assurance on is what is
our capacity to actually do this, given the

00:27:17.870 --> 00:27:20.820
resources that we have got?
Simon Stevens: Well, I think the point Malcolm

00:27:20.820 --> 00:27:26.770
is we do not have a capacity and nor do the
sort of NHS national bodies collectively to

00:27:26.770 --> 00:27:31.490
do this everywhere all at once, so that is
why we are basically cutting our cloth accordingly

00:27:31.490 --> 00:27:35.190
and saying, Look, there are these two groups
of communities that we think we can intensively

00:27:35.190 --> 00:27:41.320
work with beginning next year', supported
frankly by the £450 million of transformation

00:27:41.320 --> 00:27:46.810
funds that we have been able to secure from
the government, which gives us a fighting

00:27:46.810 --> 00:27:49.560
chance, which when we last met we did not
have under our belt.

00:27:49.560 --> 00:27:54.280
Sir Malcolm Grant: Yes, but we have got the
capacity to concentrate on those two groups

00:27:54.280 --> 00:27:57.120
to the extent that we would wish.
Simon Stevens: Yes.

00:27:57.120 --> 00:28:00.550
Sir Malcolm Grant: Okay. Margaret.
Margaret Casely Hayford: A couple of points.

00:28:00.550 --> 00:28:04.660
First of all, it is very clear that we will
be learning as we go along, and I think that

00:28:04.660 --> 00:28:11.660
what we need to ensure that the learning is
not lost is to ensure that 
that we are using those at the Vanguard to

00:28:22.440 --> 00:28:29.440
help shore up the messaging and the learning.
So, the second point is that, as Ed quite

00:28:35.080 --> 00:28:42.080
rightly said, we are going to need to make
sure that the workforce is sufficiently flexible,

00:28:43.270 --> 00:28:50.270
and that is quite an ask. So leadership supporting
workforce to make the changes is going to

00:28:52.610 --> 00:28:59.610
be absolutely critical, because that flexibility
that is required in some cases will be counterintuitive,

00:29:01.990 --> 00:29:08.990
because it is new, it is novel, so one needs
to be very supportive of those facing change.

00:29:09.570 --> 00:29:16.570
Then the last point goes back again to a point
that Ed made about the potential for porosity

00:29:18.320 --> 00:29:25.320
in the system. I am absolutely delighted that
the word 'alignment' appears so often in

00:29:26.670 --> 00:29:33.670
the guidance at the top. What is going to
be critical here is alignment in assurance

00:29:35.300 --> 00:29:42.300
processes as well, and so that when we are
seeking assurance those enterprises that are

00:29:47.520 --> 00:29:54.520
seeking assurance are not actually asking
for separate aspects of assurance that will

00:29:54.700 --> 00:29:59.460
create a burden, because otherwise people
working within the system will become weighed

00:29:59.460 --> 00:30:04.620
down by it. So where we are integrated, we
should be integrated in the way we seek assurance

00:30:04.620 --> 00:30:11.620
back, and that should be far more structured.
Simon Stevens: So, I think this is an interesting

00:30:11.920 --> 00:30:17.280
avenue of enquiry we should come back to actually,
if not in the next meeting, the meeting after,

00:30:17.280 --> 00:30:22.290
because obviously that will be the point at
which particularly the TDA, Monitor and ourselves

00:30:22.290 --> 00:30:27.870
will be taking a look at what is being planned
locally and having to make those kind of judgements.

00:30:27.870 --> 00:30:33.740
I think the reality is that TDA will continue
to have a specific set of expectations of

00:30:33.740 --> 00:30:39.770
the providers for which they have responsibility
that do not contradict but are separate from

00:30:39.770 --> 00:30:44.950
the oversight that we need of the plans coming
out of CCGs.

00:30:44.950 --> 00:30:51.710
Where I think it gets trickier is where you
have divergent expectations in the local health

00:30:51.710 --> 00:30:56.450
community. Frankly some divergence may be
entirely legitimate, but at the very least

00:30:56.450 --> 00:31:03.450
I think what we want to see is converged plan
Bs. So if one group thinks that our intention

00:31:05.190 --> 00:31:10.140
is to do this for the year and that is different
than what some of the players are doing, that

00:31:10.140 --> 00:31:15.480
could be okay, but if reality does not turn
out in the way that one group thinks, then

00:31:15.480 --> 00:31:20.200
what is the plan B to ensure that it will
work? This particularly will show up in the

00:31:20.200 --> 00:31:23.500
context of the Better Care Fund assumptions
around emergency admissions next year, for

00:31:23.500 --> 00:31:28.490
example, so I think we will need to come back
to this conversation in the New Year.

00:31:28.490 --> 00:31:32.130
Sir Malcolm Grant: On your first point of
structured advocacy, we will certainly be

00:31:32.130 --> 00:31:36.880
looking to build that into the expectations
of those Vanguard sites, that they will have

00:31:36.880 --> 00:31:42.180
a role to play not just in proving concepts
locally but they will then be able to support

00:31:42.180 --> 00:31:47.730
the subsequent waves of early adopters.
Margaret Casely Hayford: Thank you.

00:31:47.730 --> 00:31:52.840
Sir Malcolm Grant: Bruce.
Sir Bruce Keogh: Much of the discussion has

00:31:52.840 --> 00:31:59.840
revolved around how the planning guidance
is in many ways a response to what people

00:32:00.020 --> 00:32:07.020
have asked for, and that is also true in the
clinical arena which Ian alluded to. So clinical

00:32:08.080 --> 00:32:13.170
colleagues in the NHS are always looking for
ways to improve services, and of course one

00:32:13.170 --> 00:32:20.170
of our jobs is to help them do that, and as
a consequence of that we have kind of focused

00:32:20.390 --> 00:32:27.390
our endeavour around outcomes, trying to improve
outcomes in the NHS and we do that in five

00:32:27.840 --> 00:32:30.240
areas.
So first is we ask ourselves: what are we

00:32:30.240 --> 00:32:36.070
doing to help improve premature death? What
are we doing to help people with chronic and

00:32:36.070 --> 00:32:43.070
long term disease to have a better experience
of that? What are we doing to ensure that

00:32:44.790 --> 00:32:50.240
people who require short episodes of care
are getting better outcomes? How are we ensuring

00:32:50.240 --> 00:32:54.960
that patients have a better experience of
that care? Importantly, what are we doing

00:32:54.960 --> 00:33:00.900
in terms of ensuring that the way we deliver
our services is safe?

00:33:00.900 --> 00:33:07.520
Against those five areas in which we assess
ourselves, we have engaged with clinicians

00:33:07.520 --> 00:33:14.270
in the NHS to say, Where do you think we
can have some really, really big gains?' So,

00:33:14.270 --> 00:33:19.200
we have consulted with medical and nursing
directors, royal colleges, specialist associations,

00:33:19.200 --> 00:33:24.920
the Sepsis Trust, the ombudsman and others,
and what has emerged out of that discussion

00:33:24.920 --> 00:33:30.430
has been we think two areas where we can make
significant improvement are treating people

00:33:30.430 --> 00:33:33.580
with sepsis  and I will describe what that
is in a moment  and treating people who

00:33:33.580 --> 00:33:40.580
have problems with emerging kidney disease.
Sepsis is where germs get into the blood and

00:33:43.400 --> 00:33:50.110
if they multiply in the blood people become
really very, very sick; it has an impact on

00:33:50.110 --> 00:33:56.190
many of their body organs including their
kidneys, and they can deteriorate quite rapidly

00:33:56.190 --> 00:34:03.190
and die. Now, that death in many cases can
be avoidable if people are alert to the symptoms

00:34:06.350 --> 00:34:12.379
of sepsis and then give the appropriate antibiotics
very quickly, and this is a problem which

00:34:12.379 --> 00:34:18.280
is being tackled around the world at the moment
with enthusiasm, and we intend to do exactly

00:34:18.280 --> 00:34:25.280
the same. So that is the aim of what is outlined
in the planning guidance, and indeed we have

00:34:26.629 --> 00:34:30.899
support around the system for this, and indeed
the Secretary of State will be putting more

00:34:30.899 --> 00:34:37.899
flesh on the bones on this later in the week.
Sir Malcolm Grant: Victor.

00:34:38.110 --> 00:34:42.510
Victor Adebowale: It is just a thought really,
which is that this is a significant challenge

00:34:42.510 --> 00:34:48.030
to the signals we send out into the system
about how we measure what is important which

00:34:48.030 --> 00:34:55.030
are going to shift. System changes like this,
you start with some fantastic ideas, really

00:34:55.700 --> 00:35:00.490
powerful notions of what we should be doing
and how we should be doing it, and then are

00:35:00.490 --> 00:35:06.090
killed because business as usual basically
just sends out the same signals through the

00:35:06.090 --> 00:35:11.250
information that we ask for, the bureaucracy,
the systems, all that. I am not looking for

00:35:11.250 --> 00:35:18.170
a response now, but I am just interested in
how what we are saying in the planning guidance

00:35:18.170 --> 00:35:20.960
and in the Forward View and in the Forward
View planning guidance  I know it all links

00:35:20.960 --> 00:35:27.960
 actually will shift how we and what we
measure that gives this Board, and others,

00:35:28.920 --> 00:35:32.400
line of sight to what is actually happening
on the ground.

00:35:32.400 --> 00:35:37.390
There has been a number of allusions to it,
but I think it is quite a significant signal

00:35:37.390 --> 00:35:44.390
into the system, because my view is that the
NHS has got used to episodic change and so

00:35:45.870 --> 00:35:49.780
people survive the change because they know
there will be another one coming along. This

00:35:49.780 --> 00:35:56.780
is very different; this is about move to a
continuous change process, and the question

00:35:57.930 --> 00:36:03.850
is how do we then change from significant
signals into the system, which is what it

00:36:03.850 --> 00:36:10.140
is that we measure, how we measure it, how
do we co create an outcomes framework that

00:36:10.140 --> 00:36:13.990
makes sense, not just to us but to the people
that actually deliver the front end and the

00:36:13.990 --> 00:36:18.720
citizens? I think that is a significant challenge
which we have not really got our heads around

00:36:18.720 --> 00:36:25.720
yet, but I think we will need to fairly quickly
because the danger of saying one thing and

00:36:26.790 --> 00:36:30.230
doing another is frankly more dangerous than
doing nothing at all.

00:36:30.230 --> 00:36:33.090
Sir Malcolm Grant: Ian, would you care to
respond?

00:36:33.090 --> 00:36:36.800
Victor Adebowale: I am not looking for a response.
I am actually disagreeing with what I have

00:36:36.800 --> 00:36:37.270
just said. I was not trying to put anybody
on the spot.

00:36:37.270 --> 00:36:43.000
Ian Dodge: Absolutely, and I think that your
point about how do you translate a hugely

00:36:43.000 --> 00:36:50.000
ambitious vision into the actual conversations
and behaviours of individuals at all levels

00:36:52.270 --> 00:36:59.270
in the system, that are fully aligned with
the scale of that ambition and that method

00:36:59.270 --> 00:37:03.670
and spirit
Victor Adebowale: Sorry, I want to stop you;

00:37:03.670 --> 00:37:08.000
I am moving beyond the conversation to the
measures, because I know we will have the

00:37:08.000 --> 00:37:11.590
conversations. The risk is that what will
then happen is business as usual in terms

00:37:11.590 --> 00:37:16.790
of what we measure. That is what I was referring
to; it is actually what we measure, the infrastructure

00:37:16.790 --> 00:37:21.880
that surrounds that, that I am talking about,
rather than the conversations.

00:37:21.880 --> 00:37:28.130
Ian Dodge: Yes, and I think there is a profound
challenge which the planning guidance tries

00:37:28.130 --> 00:37:35.130
to address, which is to say that we still
have a set of very clear obligations to the

00:37:35.430 --> 00:37:42.430
people of England in relation to delivering
existing core performance standards set out

00:37:42.670 --> 00:37:48.340
in the NHS constitution as well as a need
to make progress against the five outcome

00:37:48.340 --> 00:37:55.340
framework domains that Bruce has alluded to
through a process of transformation, including

00:37:57.540 --> 00:38:04.540
developing new models of care. I think the
message that we will be looking to set out

00:38:05.400 --> 00:38:12.010
in the planning guidance is that neither of
those two aspects, the operational performance

00:38:12.010 --> 00:38:18.680
against the existing metrics nor what we need
to do to ensure that we have a better and

00:38:18.680 --> 00:38:25.680
sustainable NHS for the long term, are optional,
and that we will need to continue to make

00:38:27.210 --> 00:38:34.210
good progress on both of those during 2015/2016.
That is a major challenge to the service,

00:38:35.280 --> 00:38:37.220
but I think it is the reality of where we
are.

00:38:37.220 --> 00:38:41.790
Sir Malcolm Grant: Right, well I think it
might be an appropriate time at which to draw

00:38:41.790 --> 00:38:48.440
this item to a close, but to conclude on the
following terms. The first is that, as I understand

00:38:48.440 --> 00:38:55.390
it, the Board approve the principles and the
style of approach and the concentration on

00:38:55.390 --> 00:39:02.390
underpinning excellence and reinforcing the
more challenged systems, whilst not in any

00:39:03.230 --> 00:39:08.420
way relaxing its expectation on performance
across the system as a whole.

00:39:08.420 --> 00:39:15.420
I think secondly the Board would wish to see
the Five Year Forward View as a significant

00:39:15.480 --> 00:39:20.860
item in all items of business coming to the
Board in the future. It is not a standalone

00:39:20.860 --> 00:39:25.860
thing that is bolted to the side of what we
do; it is a core part of everything that we

00:39:25.860 --> 00:39:32.860
take through. Thirdly, I think we want to
be able to measure what is occurring to have

00:39:34.310 --> 00:39:41.110
good line of sight to what is occurring, and
to ensure that the collaboration, coordination

00:39:41.110 --> 00:39:45.030
and partnership that we have established with
the other arm's length bodies remains absolutely

00:39:45.030 --> 00:39:51.420
rock solid and to do what we can on that.
I think we want to be assured always that

00:39:51.420 --> 00:39:57.260
we have the resources and the capacity to
deliver what we are promising to deliver as

00:39:57.260 --> 00:40:04.260
an organisation, and I think finally we are
willing under those circumstances to delegate

00:40:05.010 --> 00:40:12.010
to the Chief Executive and the Executive Team
the final wording of the guidance, which has

00:40:12.430 --> 00:40:19.430
of course to be coordinated with our other
partners. Can you just tell us what is the

00:40:19.600 --> 00:40:24.820
timing that you anticipate for publication?
Ian Dodge: We are aiming for this Friday.

00:40:24.820 --> 00:40:30.560
Sir Malcolm Grant: Okay. So what we have today
is a summary. There is more detailed guidance

00:40:30.560 --> 00:40:35.410
which will be finely attuned with our partners
and published on Friday. Is the Board content

00:40:35.410 --> 00:40:39.109
to proceed on that basis? We are. Good, thank
you very much.

